# Service lifecycle logs (anti-flake clause 5)

A support engineer or auditor opening Event Viewer (or `journalctl -u
pim-offline-agent`, or `log show` on macOS) must see a **three-record
story** for every service lifetime: started, stopping, stopped — with
enough context to answer which version was running against which
endpoint, what was in flight when stop arrived, and whether the drain
finished inside budget. Generic `PROCESS_STARTED` / `PROCESS_STOPPED`
records answer none of those.

## The rule

> **Every binary registered as a Windows service, a systemd unit, or a
> launchd job emits, at `tracing::Level::INFO`, three records —
> `service.started`, `service.stopping`, `service.stopped` — to BOTH the
> Windows Event Log (via `pim-windows-oplog`) AND the rolling `tracing`
> log. The Windows Event Log entries use IDs from the lifecycle range
> `41001-41099`. The payload shape for each record is fixed by this
> rule.**

`PROCESS_STARTED` (41001) / `PROCESS_STOPPED` (41002) still mark the
generic process boundary for non-service binaries. Services emit the
richer triplet **in addition**.

## Event ID allocation (lifecycle range 41001-41099)

| ID | Constant (in `pim-windows-oplog::ids`) | Emitted by |
| --- | --- | --- |
| 41001 | `PROCESS_STARTED` | Every binary (existing) |
| 41002 | `PROCESS_STOPPED` | Every binary (existing) |
| 41003 | `STARTUP_FAILED` | Every binary (existing) |
| 41004 | `OPERATIONAL_WARNING` | Every binary (existing) |
| 41005 | `OPERATIONAL_ERROR` | Every binary (existing) |
| **41010** | **`SERVICE_STARTED`** | Services only |
| **41011** | **`SERVICE_STOPPING`** | Services only |
| **41012** | **`SERVICE_STOPPED`** | Services only |

41006-41009 reserved for future generic-process records; 41013-41099 for
future service-lifecycle records. A new ID requires bumping the message
catalog (`pim-windows-oplog-messages/oplog_messages.mc`) **and**
registering it in `declared_event_ids()`.

## Required payload — `service.started` (41010)

Emitted as the first action after `pim_shutdown` handler installation
completes and BEFORE any long-running task spawns. Both the Event Log
entry and the `tracing::info!` record carry these fields.

| Field | Type | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `service` | string | program id (`pim-offline-server`, `pim-offline-agent`, …) |
| `pid` | u32 | `std::process::id()` |
| `exe` | string | `std::env::current_exe()` |
| `version` | string | `env!("CARGO_PKG_VERSION")` |
| `git_sha` | string | `option_env!("PIM_BUILD_GIT_SHA").unwrap_or("unknown")` |
| `config_store_kind` | string | `"registry-hklm"` / `"libsecret+toml"` / `"keychain+plist"` |
| `service_uri` | string | primary listening URL (server) or peer URL (agent); empty if N/A |
| `machine_guid` | string | manifest `MACHINE_GUID` if set, else empty (R6) |
| `fips_posture` | string | `pim_crypto_rustls_bridge::fips_status()` rendered, e.g. `"validated:4759"` |
| `warnings_count` | u32 | `runtime_status.warnings.len()` at startup (R7) |
| `tenant_count` | u32 | server only; agents omit |

## Required payload — `service.stopping` (41011)

Emitted from the shutdown handler the moment the shutdown channel fires,
BEFORE the drain begins. This is the record that answers "what was in
flight when stop arrived?".

| Field | Type | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `service` | string | as above |
| `reason` | string | `"signal:SIGTERM"` / `"signal:SIGINT"` / `"scm:stop"` / `"scm:preshutdown"` / `"scm:shutdown"` / `"close-requested"` / `"internal:fatal"` |
| `pid` | u32 | `std::process::id()` |
| `uptime_s` | u64 | seconds since `service.started` |
| `last_sync_age_s` | u64 | seconds since last successful sync (`u64::MAX` if never) — agent only |
| `consecutive_failures` | u32 | scheduler failure counter — agent only |
| `in_flight_requests` | array | `{ url, elapsed_ms }` per pending outbound request; URL redacted via `pim_app_config::windows_oplog::redact::redact_url` |
| `inflight_count` | u32 | `in_flight_requests.len()` |

## Required payload — `service.stopped` (41012)

Emitted as the very last `tracing::info!` and `pim_windows_oplog::emit`
before `std::process::exit` (or before `main` returns).

| Field | Type | Source |
| --- | --- | --- |
| `service` | string | as above |
| `exit_code` | i32 | value passed to `std::process::exit` (`0` for clean drain) |
| `drain_total_ms` | u64 | wall clock from `service.stopping` to this record |
| `drain_steps` | array | per `pim_shutdown::drain::bounded` step: `{ name, elapsed_ms, outcome: "ok"｜"timeout"｜"error" }` |
| `aborted_steps` | u32 | count of `drain_steps[].outcome != "ok"` |
| `platform_budget_ms` | u64 | platform drain budget for this signal class (5_000 SCM_STOP, 180_000 PRESHUTDOWN, …) — the literal value from [`shutdown-compliance.mdc`](shutdown-compliance.mdc) |

## Forbidden constructs

- A service whose only lifecycle record is `PROCESS_STARTED` /
  `PROCESS_STOPPED`.
- `tracing::info!("service started")` without the structured field set
  above. The message string is the operator summary; the fields are the
  SIEM payload. Both must be present.
- A `service.stopping` record that omits `in_flight_requests`.
- A `service.stopped` record with `exit_code = 0` when any drain step's
  outcome is `"timeout"` or `"error"`. Use
  `pim_shutdown::drain::ExitCode::DRAIN_TIMEOUT` (conventionally `75`,
  `sysexits.h` EX_TEMPFAIL).
- A Windows Event Log emit that bypasses `pim-windows-oplog` (direct
  `ReportEventW`) — the shared crate keeps the message catalog honest.
- Emitting `service.started` BEFORE the `pim_shutdown` handler is
  installed. Handler first, then the lifecycle log.

## Worked violation → fix

**Violation:** `main()` calls `tracing::info!("starting
pim-offline-agent")`, awaits `ctrl_c()`, calls `scheduler.shutdown()`,
returns `Ok(())`. No version, no reason, no drain elapsed — Event Viewer
shows only a generic SCM start/stop pair, useless for fleet forensics.

**Fix:** install the shutdown handler first; emit
`pim_windows_oplog::emit(ids::SERVICE_STARTED, …)` plus the matching
`tracing::info!(op = "service.started", …)` with every field in the
table above; on shutdown emit `SERVICE_STOPPING` with `reason` and
`http.snapshot_inflight()`; after `scheduler.drain(PLATFORM_BUDGET)`
emit `SERVICE_STOPPED` with `drain_steps`, deriving `exit_code = 75`
when any step did not return `"ok"`.

